VeridianOS: socat copies names into fixed-size fields with their bounds

GCC (-Wstringop-truncation) reported four strncpy() calls; two are real
defects, two only stated their intent badly:

- xio-ip6.c: the interface of an IPv6 multicast/scope option was copied
  from a 512-byte parse buffer into a 16-byte (IF_NAMESIZE) allocation
  with strncpy(), unterminated when the name was 16 characters or more;
  ifindex() then read past the allocation. A name that long is now
  rejected ("interface name too long"), as no interface can have it.
- xio-socket.c: a temporary abstract UNIX socket name was copied at
  sun_path + 1 with sun_path's full size as the bound, one byte past the
  field. The bound is now the room after that first byte.
- xio-unix.c: a path longer than sun_path is truncated on purpose (socat
  warns), and Linux does not need the field terminated; the copy is now
  an explicit clear and bounded memcpy, the same bytes as before.
- xio-interface.c: the name was already validated as an existing
  interface (so shorter than IFNAMSIZ); it is now copied with room for
  its terminator.

--- a/xio-interface.c
+++ b/xio-interface.c
@@ -120,7 +120,8 @@
    if (rc < 0)
       return rc;
 
-   strncpy(sfd->para.interface.name, ifname, IFNAMSIZ);
+   strncpy(sfd->para.interface.name, ifname, IFNAMSIZ-1);
+   sfd->para.interface.name[IFNAMSIZ-1] = '\0';
    _xiointerface_get_iff(sfd->fd, ifname, &sfd->para.interface.save_iff);
    _xiointerface_apply_iff(sfd->fd, ifname, sfd->para.interface.iff_opts);
    if (_interface_retrieve_vlan(sfd, opts) < 0)
